A||||11||Mach10||Need a "bubblegum & Shoelace" Solution||||8||
Z||000000||Mach10||05-31-2001||10:30 PM||||It appears that my EGR valve is on the fritz. Idle jumps up and down (not rough, just 1300 one moment, 900 the next. When I plug the vacuum hose, the idle smooths out. <BR>My problem is, I'm still not getting the gas mileage I'd like to see, and a new EGR won't arrive for a week, as it's on order. What's the best way of preserving some semblance of fuel economy in the meantime? It's an 86 V6 Auto SE.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||05-31-2001||10:41 PM||||Hey Mach!<BR>Plug the hose and fergedaboudit!<BR>cancel the new egr, as it is useless!<BR>I am getting 28 or 29 mpg without the egr...<BR>crash...<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>It appears that my EGR valve is on the fritz. Idle jumps up and down (not rough, just 1300 one moment, 900 the next. When I plug the vacuum hose, the idle smooths out. <BR>My problem is, I'm still not getting the gas mileage I'd like to see, and a new EGR won't arrive for a week, as it's on order. What's the best way of preserving some semblance of fuel economy in the meantime? It's an 86 V6 Auto SE.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jerpro||05-31-2001||10:41 PM||||take the EGR out and either leave it like that or temporarily seal it until the other one gets there. The check engine light will go on but hey! it's only for a week!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||05-31-2001||11:00 PM||||I'm thinking that the EGR valve may be partially stuck open, then, as I am burning too much fuel, and I'm still exhibiting some symptoms of a bad EGR: poor starting, excessive cranking, occasionally stalling immediately after cold startup. None of these things happen WITH the EGR connected. But I do get the loping idle. Any thoughts?<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000004||SteveJ||06-01-2001||09:26 AM||||The EGR valve lowers combustion temps in the chamber reducing formation of nitrogen oxides which keeps our air cleaner. A result of the lower temps is longer life of the valves. Also keep in mind the air fuel mix is calibrated with the beleif that the EGR is functioning. I wouldn't run a car without one for long periods. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-01-2001||09:58 AM||||The temp fixes are:<P>pull the vacuum tube off the muffin onthe egr and put a golf tee in it. If the car is ok then leave it like that till the new egr comes in<P>  If the EGR is stuck open that wont fix it, then you need to loosen the 10mm bolts on the intake plenium (take the dist cap off first) and slide a piece of tin or sheet metal under the opening and tighten it back down - that will stop the intake from sucking air through the stuck open egr until the new one comes in.<P>  the problem with simply leaving your car like this is, when you let off the gas the ECM tells the EGR to open, and it advances the timing to compensate for the new condition. If the egr is blocked your engine will ping and sputter - not a good thing.<P>  when you replace the egr check the metal tube carefully - look for black soot areas that indicate a leak - esp near the top end. I brazed mine to fix a crack up there in the tube, so far its holding together ok.<P>  You can get a new tube from <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/</A> <P>the part number for the V6 from 85 to 87 is: 10137017 - they have em for $49 + shipping.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-01-2001||10:05 AM||||BTW its very easy to braze small stuff like this - goto home depot and get a swirl port torch - the ones with the fat nozzels - and get a pack of brazing rods coated with flux, and a bottle of map gas (the yellow bottle - propane will not be hot enough to braze with).<P>  you can get the whole setup for about $30 to $40, and you can braze all kinds of stuff with it - I just brazed the holes in my cruise control vacuum can closed and repainted it the other night.<P>if they have brazing flux pick up a can of that too - the coating on the rods is not enough sometimes.<P>  You can also use it to do body work on those other kinds of cars, you know - the ones with body panels that rust (c:||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||06-01-2001||09:58 PM||||Thanks. Seems to me that the valve is sticking open, somewhat, and screwing up my mixtures, forcing the ECM (via O2) to richen things up again... I'll have to wait for the new EGR. I think I'll leave it plugged in, and deal with the rough idle.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Fierowrecker||06-01-2001||11:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Thanks. Seems to me that the valve is sticking open, somewhat, and screwing up my mixtures, forcing the ECM (via O2) to richen things up again... I'll have to wait for the new EGR. I think I'll leave it plugged in, and deal with the rough idle.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The cure for a stuck egr valve is to tap on the pintle lightly with a hammer...<BR>You will have carbon coming out, so wear safety glasses...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||06-02-2001||03:32 AM||||Anyone else here catch the irony of a guy called "Fierowrecker" telling me to hit my car with a hammer?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually, that sounds VERY much like a Jaguar repair trick. If it works on a 30-year old bi-metal FED valve, it might work on this. I was actually going to take the damn thing apart and clean it out, see if I couldn't fix it. The part's so cheap though, that I might as well replace it now, while it's apart. I just have to wait till it comes in   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-02-2001||09:58 AM||||the egr itself is cheap - the weird thing is that the stupid tube is $90 at most places.<P>  treat the tube as if its made of glass when you take it off, bend it as little as possible - the stock tubes are very brittle and fragile after they have been used for a while.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||06-02-2001||01:22 PM||||Ugh, don't even get me started. I can't seem to find a place that can even GET the damn tube. Trust me, when that thing comes off, It's staying at the "Lombard." (Most expensive hotel in Winnipeg. Next to Super6, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )||||reg||8
